<h2 id="13c-hq-workflows-and-your-clients-information">13C. HQ workflows and your clients' information</h2>
<p>HQ's workflows plan your work, write drafts and make files for your business, and its Pipeline keeps your leads and clients. This section applies whenever you put information about other people into HQ: your clients and leads, contacts you import, businesses a workflow finds for you, docs in your Brain, what your connections bring in, and chats on your site.</p>
<ul class="bul">
<li><strong>What you put in.</strong> Add information about other people only if you're allowed to use it for your business, and only what the work needs. Import contacts only to reach people who know you. Where the law requires it, tell your clients what you keep and why, get the consent it requires, and answer their requests about their information. You're the business they deal with, and we help as this section describes.</li>
<li><strong>Keep sensitive details out.</strong> Don't put government ID numbers, full card or bank numbers, passwords or medical records into HQ. Keep health details out too, except where a workflow asks for them: the PAR-Q+ result in the personal training workflow and the foods to leave out in the meal prep workflow. Keep full health forms and allergy details in your own records. HQ isn't built for health records covered by HIPAA, so don't use it for them, and we don't sign business associate agreements. If a health data law applies to your business, like Washington's My Health My Data Act, the notices and consents it requires are yours to give and get.</li>
<li><strong>Children.</strong> If you teach or look after children, keep to a first name and what the parent needs, and write to the parent, never the child.</li>
<li><strong>We're your service provider.</strong> We handle your clients' and leads' information only on your behalf and only to provide HQ to you, following these Terms and what you do in HQ, which are your instructions to us. We don't sell or share it, use it for advertising or to train AI models, combine it with information from other members, or contact the people it's about on our own. People at 127 keep it confidential and look at it only to help when you ask, to fix a problem or to enforce these Terms. Our hosting and AI providers handle it for us under contracts that protect it, as our <a href="/privacy#7b-hq-workflows-and-your-clients-information">Privacy Policy</a> explains. We protect it with reasonable security, handle it in line with the privacy laws that apply to us, and will tell you if we can no longer meet these commitments. You can stop our use of it at any time by deleting it. Ask us, and we'll tell you in writing how we keep these promises.</li>
<li><strong>Requests about it.</strong> You can delete a lead, a run, a file, a repeat or a win in HQ yourself. Deleting a lead also deletes the drafts and answers HQ wrote for it and what workflows saved under its name, and takes its name off your wins. To delete anything else, or to get a copy of what HQ holds about one person, email us at hello@127group.io and we'll do it promptly. If someone asks us about information you keep in HQ, we'll pass the request to you or tell them to ask you.</li>
<li><strong>Nothing goes out without your OK.</strong> HQ never sends anything to your clients or leads on its own. It writes drafts, and you send each one yourself from your own email, phone or Instagram, or, where you've connected an account that can send (like Stripe invoices), you tap OK in HQ and HQ sends that one from your account. The one exception is your site chat, which answers people who write to it (Section 13D). You're responsible for what you send and the laws on it: follow Section 13B's rules in every sales email, and don't text anyone who hasn't given you their number.</li>
<li><strong>Check what workflows make.</strong> Plans, menus, schedules, prices and drafts come from code and AI. They can be wrong, and they aren't professional advice (Sections 6 and 7). Check them before you use them with a client, especially anything about health, safety, food, money or the law.</li>
<li><strong>Wins.</strong> If you let us share a win, leave your client's name out of it.</li>
<li><strong>How long it stays.</strong> A run is deleted 180 days after it ends. Your Pipeline, files, repeats and wins stay until you delete them or your account, even after a paid plan ends. Deleting your account deletes all of it, as our Privacy Policy explains.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="13d-your-brain-ask-127-your-ai-team-site-chat-and-studio">13D. Your Brain, Ask 127, your AI team, site chat and Studio</h2>
<p>Where your plan includes them, HQ can keep your business's documents and rules (your Brain), answer and act for you in chat (Ask 127), run AI helpers that work in the accounts you connect (your AI team), answer visitors in a chat on your own website (site chat), and make images, video and voice (Studio). This section applies when you use any of them.</p>
<ul class="bul">
<li><strong>Your Brain.</strong> Add only documents, notes and rules you have the right to use, and only what your business needs. Ask HQ to draft it only from your own website or bio. They're your content (Section 9), and Section 13C applies to anything in them about other people, including what it says to keep out. Your house rules go with every AI request HQ makes for you, so keep other people's details and anything secret out of them. A doc you mark public can be quoted to anyone who uses your site chat, so mark public only what you'd put on your website.</li>
<li><strong>Nothing goes out without your OK.</strong> Ask 127 suggests actions, like starting a workflow or logging a decision, and takes each one when you tap OK. Skills and your AI team do the work you set them up for. Anything that sends, posts, publishes, charges, spends, deletes or changes something in an account you've connected waits for you to tap OK in HQ, each time. Read what you approve: your OK is your decision. An AI app you connect through your AI link can get these things ready too, but the OK has to come from you in HQ.</li>
<li><strong>AI, not people.</strong> Ask 127, your AI team, site chat and Studio use AI, so Section 6 applies to them: they can be wrong, check what they make before you rely on it or send it, and none of it is professional advice. We call the helpers on your AI team AI employees because each one has a job, but they're software that 127 runs for you. They aren't people, and they aren't your employees or ours. A helper's name describes its work; it isn't an accountant, lawyer, financial adviser or any other licensed professional.</li>
<li><strong>Your activity log.</strong> HQ records what you approve, and what Ask 127, your AI team, your schedules, your AI link and your connections do. You can see and export it in HQ.</li>
<li><strong>Credits and budgets.</strong> Ask 127, your AI team and Studio use your plan's HQ credits (Section 6). A helper stops when its budget or your allowance runs out. We never charge you for more without your OK.</li>
<li><strong>Your site chat.</strong> It answers your visitors only from the docs and details you mark public, and it tells them at the start that it's an AI working for your business and that chats are saved for you. You're the business your visitors deal with: you decide what it may say, you're responsible for its answers like anything else on your site, and your site needs a privacy notice that covers it (our site templates include a starter). Don't add it to a site or page meant for children. We run it for you as your service provider (Section 13C), and your visitors' chats are yours.</li>
<li><strong>Studio.</strong> Add a real person's photo, video or voice only if it's you, or you have that person's written permission to use their face or voice this way, and never anyone under 18. A voice or look that Studio keeps so it can make that person again can only be yours. Don't make media that imitates a real person who hasn't agreed, including celebrities and public figures, or that presents a made-up person as a real customer, client or reviewer. When Studio makes an ad with an AI-made person or voice, the ad carries a label that says so; keep it on. When any other post shows an AI-made person or voice, or a real one changed by AI, that people could take as real, label it where the law or the platform requires, and turn on the platform's AI label when you post. Each Studio tool links the usage rules of the company that makes it, and you agree to follow them too. If you connect your own account with one of those companies, its terms and your plan there also apply to what you make with it. What Studio makes is your output (Section 6), but it may resemble what others make and may not be protectable. Our Privacy Policy names the companies that make it.</li>
<li><strong>Templates, checklists and estimates.</strong> Store templates, HQ's tools (like Profit and break-even and Cash runway), setup and compliance checklists, bookkeeping categories and tax set-aside estimates are general tools, not legal, tax or accounting advice (Sections 6 and 7). Rules differ by state and situation, so have a qualified professional check anything you'll sign, file or rely on.</li>
<li><strong>Your ads.</strong> If you connect an ad account, you're the advertiser: your ads, targeting, budgets and claims are yours, and so are the ad platform's rules and charges. HQ creates new ads paused. It turns an ad on, or changes an ad, audience, budget or schedule, only when you tap OK, and it never changes your payment settings. HQ asks whether each campaign is about housing, jobs, credit or another financial product and, if it is, uses the platform's special category and the limits on targeting that come with it. It doesn't make political, election or social issue ads, or ads aimed at anyone under 18. Ads made with 127 never claim income, earnings or guaranteed results, and never use made-up reviews or people presented as customers. Have proof for any claim you add.</li>
</ul>
